@prefix atlas:   <https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/> .
@prefix prim:    <https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/primitive/> .
@prefix entry:   <https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/entry/> .
@prefix bearer:  <https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/bearer/> .
@prefix case:    <https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/case/> .
@prefix obj:     <https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/object/> .
@prefix cco:     <https://www.commoncoreontologies.org/> .
@prefix owl:     <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix dcterms: <http://purl.org/dc/terms/> .

<https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/entries/klir-2001-facets>
    a owl:Ontology ;
    owl:imports <https://w3id.org/mathematical-systems/atlas/atlas-core> ;
    rdfs:label "Atlas entry — Klir 2001, Facets of Systems Science"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Entry 001. The first entry in the catalogue, and the tradition the deck opens with."@en .


##############################################################################
# Bearer
##############################################################################

bearer:klir-2001-facets
    a cco:ont00000253 ;   # Information Bearing Entity
    rdfs:label "Klir, G. J. — Facets of Systems Science, 2nd ed. (Springer, 2001)"@en ;
    dcterms:creator "George J. Klir" ;
    dcterms:date "2001"^^xsd:gYear ;
    dcterms:identifier "ISBN 978-1-4613-5501-4" ;
    dcterms:identifier "doi:10.1007/978-1-4615-1331-5" .


##############################################################################
# Entry 001 — Klir's common-sense definition
##############################################################################

entry:klir-2001-eq-1-1
    a atlas:FormalSystemDefinition ;
    rdfs:label "Klir (2001), Facets of Systems Science, eq. (1.1)"@en ;

    atlas:statedIn bearer:klir-2001-facets ;
    atlas:sourceLocation "ch. 1, eq. (1.1), p. 5" ;

    atlas:verbatim """S = (T, R),   (1.1)

where S, T, R denote, respectively, a system, a set of things distinguished within S, and a relation (or, possibly, a set of relations) defined on T. Clearly, the thinghood and systemhood properties of S reside in T and R, respectively.""" ;

    # Presentation spans — exact substrings of the verbatim, enforced by the build.
    atlas:displayForm "S = (T, R)" ;
    atlas:displayContext "where S, T, R denote, respectively, a system, a set of things distinguished within S, and a relation (or, possibly, a set of relations) defined on T." ;

    atlas:invokesPrimitive prim:thing ,
                           prim:relation ,
                           prim:set ,
                           prim:thinghood ,
                           prim:systemhood ;

    atlas:admits case:klir-books-ordered ,
                 case:klir-books-partitioned ,
                 case:klir-population-by-birth ;

    atlas:formalisedAs "Systems/Category/ShapeKlir.lean#KlirShape" ;

    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:HVP ;
    atlas:encodedBy "Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-08-01"^^xsd:date ;

    rdfs:comment """VERIFIED: verbatim transcribed from the primary text, not paraphrased.

DELIBERATELY NOT ASSERTED: cco:ont00001808 (is about). Klir is an avowed
constructivist — on his account the relation R is defined by the modeller, not
discovered. Asserting what this definition is *about* would commit the catalogue
to a realist reading Klir does not hold. The neutrality is the point.

EXAMPLES NOTE: Klir gives no excluded example in Bunge's sense. His "a collection
of books is not a system, only a set" (p. 5, same paragraph as the included
examples) is the opening move of an argument whose conclusion is that organising
it *makes* it one, and on his own criterion — an object is a system iff it CAN BE
DESCRIBED in a form conforming to Eq. (1.1) — a bare collection is not refused but
merely not yet distinguished. Recording it as atlas:excludedExample would give it
the same form as Bunge's ontic refusal at Definition 1.1 and manufacture a symmetry
the two authors do not share; in a cases matrix it would read as agreement where
the finding is the opposite. The three included examples are grouped as Klir groups
them: his "etc." marks the variants as instances of a kind, not an enumeration of
cases. Source read 2026-08-03; see docs/proposals/P1-klir-examples.md and
docs/proposals/P3-stance-axes.md.

CENSUS NOTE: Klir's own gloss splits the definition's two slots into named
properties — thinghood in T, systemhood in R. Recorded as separate primitives
rather than folded into `thing` and `relation`, because whether they are distinct
primitives is a question the census should answer, not one the encoding should
prejudge.

ADJACENT MATERIAL, NOT YET ENTERED: Klir's self-assessment on the same page (the
definition is "too general and, consequently, of little pragmatic value" but
"encompasses all other, more specific definitions of systems") is a claim ABOUT
this definition's relation to others. It belongs in the mapping layer, not in
this entry.

NEXT TIER: the dependency quiver (tier 1) for this definition already exists in
Lean as ShapeKlir in systems-science-foundations. The two encodings must be
checked against each other before either is cited."""@en .


##############################################################################
# Cases — reified 2026-08-03 (P4). Each carries its own location and grade.
##############################################################################

case:klir-books-ordered
    a atlas:Example ;
    rdfs:label "a collection of books, ordered"@en ;
    atlas:gloss "a collection of books ordered by authors' names, publication dates, or size" ;
    atlas:verbatim "any ordering of a set is a relation defined on the set" ;
    atlas:sourceLocation "ch. 1, p. 5" ;
    atlas:instantiates obj:ordered-non-bonding ;
    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:HVP ;
    atlas:encodedBy "Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-08-03"^^xsd:date .

case:klir-books-partitioned
    a atlas:Example ;
    rdfs:label "a collection of books, partitioned"@en ;
    atlas:gloss "a collection of books partitioned by subject, publisher, or language" ;
    atlas:verbatim "every partition of a set emerges from a particular equivalence relation defined on the set" ;
    atlas:sourceLocation "ch. 1, p. 5" ;
    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:HVP ;
    atlas:encodedBy "Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-08-03"^^xsd:date .

case:klir-population-by-birth
    a atlas:Example ;
    rdfs:label "a human population, ordered by dates of birth"@en ;
    atlas:gloss "a human population ordered by dates of birth" ;
    atlas:verbatim "may be applied not only to other sets of books, but also to sets whose elements are not books" ;
    atlas:sourceLocation "ch. 1, p. 5" ;
    atlas:evidenceCode atlas:HVP ;
    atlas:encodedBy "Shingai Thornton (Claude-assisted)" ;
    atlas:encodedOn "2026-08-03"^^xsd:date ;
    rdfs:comment """The quotation licenses this case rather than describing it: Klir is making
a point about a RELATION being transferable to a different carrier set, and the human
population is his instance of it. A splice, recorded as one."""@en .
